; HYPOTHETICAL. THIS IS NOT L0 AND DOES NOT TYPE CHECK.
;
; What limit 10 constrains. Two separate things would lift it.
;
; 1. Static initialized bytes, for text that must exist before the heap does
;    or after it is gone: "heap exhausted", and "stack exhausted" in the
;    signal handler.
;
;      (static_bytes NAME "TEXT")   static storage holding TEXT's bytes
;
; 2. A way to return two results without a heap block. The allocator in
;    runtime.l0 was split into arena_fits and arena_grow_bytes only because
;    returning both answers means building a data value, which calls
;    l0_data_new. With a non-allocating data declaration, for example
;
;      (value_data NAME CTOR ...)   passed and returned in registers, never
;                                   stored in a list or another data value
;
;    the natural version would compile to no allocation:

(static_bytes heap_msg "l0: runtime error: heap exhausted\n")

(value_data Bump
  (Fits int int)                           ; address, new bump pointer
  (Grow int))                              ; chunk size to ask the OS for

(def arena_bump ((cur int) (end int) (bytes int)) Bump
  (let size (align16 bytes)
    (if (arena_fits cur end size)
        (Fits cur (add cur size))
        (Grow (arena_grow_bytes size)))))

; Of the two, only the first is needed for the runtime. Once static storage
; and stores exist (limits 01 and 02), the allocator keeps its state in
; memory and never needs to return two things; 02-global-state.l0 is written
; that way. The second is listed because it is what an L0 programmer reaches
; for first, and the recursion it causes is silent until the runtime is L0.
